#{[17592186240908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240908 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240908 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240909 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e735c8c7-7a76-5786-82b2-e7e846ae7fe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240920 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86f7c787-e5df-5794-8bba-c906aa91522f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240924 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13fd31c7-58b2-51a7-95e7-235c3524f10b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240928 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{6}-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256847 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\pi }^{2}+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0794e38d-83c6-592a-8698-603e9bbcd5de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1080 "3e+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256930 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256930 #edb/ref